  "steps": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "In Morse code, every letter is a pattern of long and short beeps. SOS is three short, three long, then three short. Tap it on your desk: short, short, short, long, long, long, short, short, short. What makes a long one feel long?"
    },
    "explore": {
      "text": "Pick any key. Tap it quickly for a short sound. Now press it and hold it down for a long sound. Then make a pattern of your own: short, short, long.",
      "instrument": "keyboard",
      "exhibit": {
        "kind": "keyboard",
        "highlight": [],
        "low": "C4",
        "high": "G4"
      },
      "goal": "Play a short sound, then a long one, then a short one."
    },
    "name": {
      "cards": [
        {
          "text": "A pattern of long and short sounds is a [[rhythm|rhythm]]. Morse code is a rhythm. So is the pattern you clap along to in a song."
        },
        {
          "text": "Rhythm sits on top of the [[pulse|pulse]]. The pulse ticks steadily underneath, and the rhythm decides how long each sound lasts. In this lesson a short sound lasts 1 [[beat|beat]] and a long sound lasts 2."
        },
        {
          "text": "To copy a rhythm by ear, keep your foot on the pulse. Tap once for every sound, long ones too. After a long sound, wait for its second beat before you tap again."
        }
      ],
      "alternative": {
        "text": "Your foot is the pulse. Your voice is the rhythm. Tap your foot steadily and say \"ta\" on each tap: ta, ta, ta. Those are short sounds. Now stretch one over two taps: \"taa-aa\". That is long. Try ta, ta, taa-aa. The foot never changes."
      }
    },
